        <!-- TEXT 1: WIDE SARGASSO SEA -->
        <h2>1️⃣ WIDE SARGASSO SEA (Jean Rhys, 1966)</h2>
        
        <h3>📖 CONTEXT & STRUCTURE</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Prequel to:</strong> <em>Jane Eyre</em> (Charlotte Brontë) - tells story of "mad woman in the attic"</li>
            <li><strong>Setting:</strong> Jamaica & Dominica, post-Emancipation Act (1834), 1840s</li>
            <li><strong>Narration:</strong> <span class="critical">Multiple 1st person narrators</span> - Antoinette (Part 1 & 3), Rochester (Part 2), Grace Poole (brief)</li>
            <li><strong>Effect of structure:</strong> Fragmented, unreliable narration → mimics Antoinette's mental breakdown</li>
            <li><strong>Genre:</strong> Gothic psychological novel, postcolonial literature</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🎨 KEY THEMES WITH NOTES</h3>
        
        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 1: <span class="identity">IDENTITY ALIENATION & RACIAL LIMINALITY</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Antoinette = <strong>Creole white</strong> (European descent born in Caribbean)</li>
            <li>Caught between worlds: <strong>Too "white" for Black Jamaicans + Too "colonial" for English whites</strong></li>
            <li>Called "white cockroach" by Black children</li>
            <li>Rochester renames her "Bertha" → <span class="critical">Erases identity completely</span></li>
        </ul>
        
        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 1:</strong> "They say when trouble comes close ranks, and so the white people did. But we were not in their ranks." (p. 17)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Shows Antoinette's social exclusion. English colonists reject her family because they're "local" whites who've "gone native." She doesn't belong anywhere = double alienation.
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 2: <span class="gender">MARRIAGE AS POWER/OPPRESSION</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Marriage to Rochester = <strong>economic transaction</strong> (he gets her £30,000 dowry)</li>
            <li>Rochester = metaphor for <span class="colonialism">colonial power</span> (takes money, land, name, freedom)</li>
            <li>Antoinette loses ALL legal rights after marriage (1840s British law)</li>
            <li>She becomes his property, imprisoned in attic</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 2:</strong> "All women, all colours, nothing but fools. Three children I have [...] but no husband, I thank my God. I keep my money." (Christophine, p. 109)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Christophine (former slave) presents alternative = <strong>female independence</strong>. She refuses marriage because she understands it = trap. Feminist critique of patriarchy.
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 3: <span class="colonialism">COLONIALISM & PSYCHOLOGICAL DESTRUCTION</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Post-Emancipation Jamaica = unstable (former slaves vs. white plantation owners)</li>
            <li>Antoinette's family = <strong>ruined by abolition</strong> (lost slave labor, wealth)</li>
            <li>Rochester comes from England for money → represents colonial exploitation</li>
            <li>He destroys her sanity through <strong>gaslighting, isolation, renaming</strong></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 3:</strong> "What am I doing in this place and who am I?" (Antoinette, p. 180)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Final disintegration of identity. Locked in English attic, called "Bertha," lost name, country, freedom, sanity. <span class="critical">Complete identity erasure</span>. Colonialism destroys psychologically, not just economically.
        </div>

        <h3>🔑 KEY LITERARY TECHNIQUES</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Fragmented narration:</strong> Mirrors Antoinette's fractured psyche</li>
            <li><strong>Gothic imagery:</strong> Dark forests, fire, mirrors, dreams → madness, entrapment</li>
            <li><strong>Symbolism:</strong> Fire (destruction + freedom), Mirrors (broken identity), Red dress (passion + danger)</li>
            <li><strong>Postcolonial rewriting:</strong> Gives voice to Brontë's silent "madwoman"</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- TEXT 2: THINGS FALL APART -->
        <h2>2️⃣ THINGS FALL APART (Chinua Achebe, 1958)</h2>
        
        <h3>📖 CONTEXT & STRUCTURE</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> <span class="critical">Counter European stereotypes of Africa</span> ("savage," "primitive")</li>
            <li><strong>Setting:</strong> Umuofia village, Igboland (Nigeria), late 1800s-early 1900s</li>
            <li><strong>Narration:</strong> <span class="critical">3rd person omniscient</span> - objective, documentary-like</li>
            <li><strong>Effect:</strong> Presents Igbo culture as <strong>complex, sophisticated civilization</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Structure:</strong> Part 1 (Igbo life before), Part 2 (Okonkwo's exile), Part 3 (colonialism arrives)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🎨 KEY THEMES WITH NOTES</h3>
        
        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 1: <span class="tradition">TRADITION VS. CHANGE / COLONIALISM ARRIVAL</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Part 1:</strong> Detailed Igbo society - justice system, religion, art, gender roles</li>
            <li><strong>Part 3:</strong> British missionaries + government officials
                <ul>
                    <li>Christianity converts outcasts first (twins, osu, women seeking escape)</li>
                    <li>Church = <span class="critical">divides community from within</span></li>
                    <li>British impose foreign government, jail, taxes</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 1:</strong> "Age was respected among his people, but achievement was revered. As the elders said, if a child washed his hands he could eat with kings." (Ch. 1)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Igbo value system = <strong>meritocracy</strong>. Anyone can rise through hard work. Okonkwo = self-made man. Shows sophisticated society values achievement.
        </div>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 2:</strong> "He has put a knife on the things that held us together and we have fallen apart." (Ch. 20)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Direct reference to title. "Knife" = colonialism cuts social cohesion. <span class="critical">Society "falls apart" from internal division, not just external force.</span>
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 2: <span class="gender">TOXIC MASCULINITY & ITS LIMITS</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Okonkwo's tragic flaw = <strong>excessive masculinity</strong> (fear of appearing weak)</li>
            <li>He values: strength, violence, hard work, wealth, many wives</li>
            <li>Results: Beats wives, kills Ikemefuna (adopted son), alienates son Nwoye</li>
            <li><span class="critical">When colonialism makes warrior-masculinity obsolete, Okonkwo has nothing left</span></li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🔑 KEY LITERARY TECHNIQUES</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Third-person omniscient:</strong> Authoritative, documentary → preserves Igbo culture</li>
            <li><strong>Proverbs:</strong> "Proverbs are the palm-oil with which words are eaten" - sophisticated oral culture</li>
            <li><strong>Tragic structure:</strong> Okonkwo's hamartia (excessive masculinity) → downfall</li>
            <li><strong>Irony:</strong> Okonkwo works whole life to avoid being weak like father → ends worse (suicide)</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- TEXT 3: INTIMATE APPAREL -->
        <h2>3️⃣ INTIMATE APPAREL (Lynn Nottage, 2003)</h2>
        
        <h3>📖 CONTEXT & STRUCTURE</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Based on:</strong> Photograph "Unidentified Negro Seamstress, ca. 1905"</li>
            <li><strong>Setting:</strong> New York City, 1905 (Lower East Side & Manhattan)</li>
            <li><strong>Form:</strong> <span class="critical">Stage play</span> - realistic, episodic scenes</li>
            <li><strong>Effect:</strong> Intimate, performance-based → emphasizes human connection through dialogue</li>
            <li><strong>Historical context:</strong> Jim Crow segregation, Great Migration beginning, women can't vote</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🎨 KEY THEMES WITH NOTES</h3>
        
        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 1: <span class="dream">THE AMERICAN DREAM & INACCESSIBILITY</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Esther's Reality:</strong> Works 18 years, saves for beauty salon, can't get loan without male sponsor</li>
            <li>Forced to marry George → he steals all savings (sewn into quilt)</li>
            <li><span class="critical">18 years of work → destroyed in one night</span></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 1:</strong> "I got a plan. Esther, I'm gonna have me a beauty parlor for colored ladies. A place where they can feel like queens." (Act 1, Scene 2)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Esther's dream = not just wealth, but <strong>dignity for Black women</strong>. Beauty salon = space of self-care, community. American Dream for marginalized = collective uplift.
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 2: <span class="gender">INTERSECTIONALITY: RACE + GENDER + CLASS</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Esther:</strong> Black + woman + working-class = <span class="critical">triple oppression</span></li>
            <li><strong>Mayme:</strong> Black + woman + sex worker → "Dreams are fun, but they ain't real"</li>
            <li><strong>Mrs. Van Buren:</strong> White + woman + rich → has wealth but NO autonomy, trapped in loveless marriage</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 2:</strong> "Friends? How we friends? When I ain't never been through your front door." (Esther to Mrs. Van Buren, Act 2, Scene 3)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Exposes <strong>limits of cross-racial female friendship</strong> under segregation. Class and race create insurmountable barriers.
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 3: <span class="language">FABRIC AS LANGUAGE / INTIMACY</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Esther creates intimate apparel = lingerie, corsets</li>
            <li>She "reads" clients through fabric needs</li>
            <li><strong>Crazy quilt:</strong> <span class="critical">Central symbol</span>
                <ul>
                    <li>Patchwork = 18 years of work</li>
                    <li>Contains all savings sewn inside</li>
                    <li>George rips it open = dream destroyed</li>
                    <li>Must start NEW quilt = resilience, starting over</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🔑 KEY LITERARY TECHNIQUES</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Stage play format:</strong> Intimate, realistic staging</li>
            <li><strong>Symbolism:</strong> Crazy quilt (American Dream), intimate apparel (hidden labor), letters (false communication)</li>
            <li><strong>Final image:</strong> "Unidentified Negro Seamstress, ca. 1905" = anonymity (history erases marginalized people), but play recovers her story</li>
            <li><strong>Semi-hopeful ending:</strong> Esther survives with dignity, female solidarity as survival</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- TEXT 4: THE GLASS MENAGERIE -->
        <h2>4️⃣ THE GLASS MENAGERIE (Tennessee Williams, 1944)</h2>
        
        <h3>📖 CONTEXT & STRUCTURE</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Subtitle:</strong> "A Memory Play"</li>
            <li><strong>Setting:</strong> St. Louis, Missouri, 1930s (Great Depression era)</li>
            <li><strong>Narration:</strong> <span class="critical">Tom = narrator AND character</span> - entire play is his memory</li>
            <li><strong>Effect:</strong> <span class="memory">Everything filtered through Tom's perspective</span> - dramatic, sentimental, NOT realistic</li>
            <li><strong>Genre:</strong> Memory play, family drama, American realism</li>
            <li><strong>Autobiographical:</strong> Based on Williams's own life (real name = Thomas, sister Rose had lobotomy)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🎨 KEY THEMES WITH NOTES</h3>
        
        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 1: <span class="memory">MEMORY AS TRAP & ESCAPE</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Tom opens play: <strong>"The play is memory"</strong> - all action takes place in his head</li>
            <li>Tom the character = feels trapped by memory, past prevents him from living</li>
            <li>Tom the narrator = STILL trapped (creating play = he hasn't escaped memories)</li>
            <li>Amanda = uses memories as <strong>veil to shield from reality</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Clings to Southern belle version of herself</li>
                    <li>17 gentleman callers in one weekend (repeated story)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Laura = retreats to past as safe haven, perfect world removed from time</li>
            <li>Jim = NOT haunted by past (only character living in present)</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 1:</strong> "Yes, I have tricks in my pocket, I have things up my sleeve. But I am the opposite of a stage magician. He gives you illusion that has the appearance of truth. I give you truth in the pleasant disguise of illusion." (Tom, opening monologue)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Tom announces he's the "author" of events. Play = "truth" filtered through memory and theatrical artifice. But Tennessee Williams is REAL author → Tom is still character in framed play. <span class="critical">Multiple layers of illusion.</span>
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 2: <span class="dream">ILLUSIONS AND DREAMS VS. REALITY</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Tom's dreams:</strong> Escape from present life
                <ul>
                    <li>Writes poetry in warehouse</li>
                    <li>Discusses joining merchant marines</li>
                    <li>Escapes into action-adventure movies every night</li>
                    <li>"Going to the movies" = also code for getting drunk</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Amanda's dreams:</strong> Desperate attempts to escape sadness → become <span class="critical">self-delusions</span>
                <ul>
                    <li>Insists Tom will be successful businessman</li>
                    <li>Projects gentleman caller fantasy onto Laura</li>
                    <li>Wears frilly dress from youth when Jim visits (becomes past version of herself)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Laura's dreams:</strong> NOT ambitions, but <strong>refuge from pain</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Glass menagerie = perfect, immortal objects she can control</li>
                    <li>Unlike Amanda, Laura DOESN'T delude herself about disabilities</li>
                    <li>She just retreats from world (park, zoo, "Jewel Box" greenhouse)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 2:</strong> "But the wonderfullest trick of all was the coffin trick...There is a trick that would come in handy for me—get me out of this two-by-four situation!" (Tom to Laura, Scene 4)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> <span class="critical">Coffin trick = Tom's life.</span> Magician escapes coffin without removing nails = Tom wants to escape apartment without hurting family. Tom feels dead, nailed in place. But he's also trapped in his own head/memories (stage = trap).
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 3: <span class="gender">ABANDONMENT & GENDER ROLES</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Father:</strong> Abandoned family, worked for telephone company, "fell in love with long distances"
                <ul>
                    <li>Only contact = postcard "Hello—Goodbye!"</li>
                    <li>His portrait dominates living room (looms over play)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Amanda's fear:</strong> Tom will abandon like father did
                <ul>
                    <li>This makes her MORE controlling, which pushes Tom away</li>
                    <li>Relentlessly pushes Laura to get business skills OR marry</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Jim's abandonment:</strong> Play's dramatic climax
                <ul>
                    <li>Dances with Laura, kisses her, breaks glass unicorn</li>
                    <li>Reveals he's engaged to Betty → shatters Laura's hopes</li>
                    <li>He exits back to his real life, leaving Wingfields behind</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Tom's abandonment:</strong> Entire play = tension between love for family and desire for freedom
                <ul>
                    <li>He physically leaves at end</li>
                    <li>BUT creating the play = he never truly left (paradox)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote-box">
            <strong>QUOTE 3:</strong> "Go, then! Go to the moon—you selfish dreamer!" (Amanda's last line to Tom)
        </div>
        <div class="analysis-box">
            <strong>Analysis:</strong> Amanda accuses Tom of being selfish dreamer, but SHE is also guilty. She lives in memories/illusions. Tom takes her words literally and leaves for good, following father's path.
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 15px 0;"><strong>Theme 4: <span class="identity">ESCAPE - TWO DIRECTIONS</span></strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Amanda & Laura:</strong> Escape FROM real world INTO memory/dreams</li>
            <li><strong>Tom:</strong> Wants to escape FROM memory/dreams INTO real world</li>
            <li><strong>Tom's escape mechanisms (all cyclical):</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>Fire escape (literal & figurative temporary release)</li>
                    <li>Movies (fantasy on screen, but must return home)</li>
                    <li>Smoking (controls his own flame vs. domestic fires)</li>
                    <li>Alcohol (bottles in pockets)</li>
                    <li>Poetry/typewriter (mental escape from warehouse)</li>
                    <li>Merchant marines (fantasizes about leaving landlocked Midwest)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Paradox:</strong> All Tom's escapes trap him in cycles. "I'm leading a double life" (Scene 3)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🔑 KEY SYMBOLS - TGM</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Glass menagerie:</strong> Laura's fragility, otherworldliness, tragic beauty
                <ul>
                    <li>Represents Laura's imaginative world, haven from society</li>
                    <li>Vulnerability of memory/dreams: one false move = entire universe shatters</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Glass unicorn:</strong> Laura herself - beautiful, magical, unique
                <ul>
                    <li>When Jim breaks horn off → becomes normal horse (not magical creature)</li>
                    <li>Laura gives broken unicorn to Jim as souvenir</li>
                    <li>Signals impossibility for Jim/Laura to be together (she can't exist in his world without breaking)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Blue Roses:</strong> Jim's mispronunciation of "pleurosis" (Laura's childhood illness)
                <ul>
                    <li>Turns Laura's defect into asset: unusual qualities = special, not debilitating</li>
                    <li>Reference to Williams's sister Rose (underwent lobotomy)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Fire escape:</strong> Tom's literal/figurative temporary release
                <ul>
                    <li>He smokes there = lighting own flame (controls destiny vs. consumed by family)</li>
                    <li>Foreshadows eventual departure</li>
                    <li>When Laura forced onto fire escape, she stumbles (bound to Wingfield world)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Music:</strong> "The Glass Menagerie" theme repeats throughout
                <ul>
                    <li>Laura & Amanda associate music with absent Mr. Wingfield (left them his Victrola)</li>
                    <li>Victrola = Laura's auditory escape</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Movies:</strong> Tom's escape into alternate narratives
                <ul>
                    <li>BUT: temporary, false escape (must always return)</li>
                    <li>Also code for drinking</li>
                    <li>Commentary on theater itself (audience escapes reality by watching play)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Typewriter:</strong> Different meanings for each character
                <ul>
                    <li>Laura: confines of business world she escapes from</li>
                    <li>Amanda: Laura's failure + Tom's failure to commit to warehouse</li>
                    <li>Tom: means of escape (composes manuscripts)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>🔑 KEY LITERARY TECHNIQUES - TGM</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Memory play structure:</strong> Tom = both narrator and character
                <ul>
                    <li>Direct address to audience</li>
                    <li>Non-realistic, dreamlike, sentimental</li>
                    <li>Tom controls lighting, music to manipulate emotional tone</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Elaborate stage directions:</strong> Williams provides interpretive grip
                <ul>
                    <li>Descriptions make play readable as text, not just performance</li>
                    <li>Screen on stage displays words/images (keeps audience aware it's symbolic)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Lighting symbolism:</strong> Multiple light sources = different aspects of hope/melancholy
                <ul>
                    <li>Electric light goes out (Tom didn't pay bill)</li>
                    <li>Candelabra (romantic, dreamlike)</li>
                    <li>Moonlight (romantic hope + foolish dreams)</li>
                    <li>Tom's cigarettes (control, private spotlight)</li>
                    <li>Jim's cigarette (confidence, keeps spotlight around him)</li>
                    <li>Final line: "For nowadays the world is lit by lightning!" (violent, harsh light)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Autobiographical elements:</strong> 
                <ul>
                    <li>Williams's real name = Thomas ("Tennessee" from father's home state)</li>
                    <li>Sister Rose = delicate, mentally ill, had lobotomy (Laura = based on her)</li>
                    <li>Mother Evelina = Southern belle (like Amanda)</li>
                    <li>Father = tyrannical, frequently absent</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>

