        <!-- PARALLELS SECTION -->
        <h2>🔗 PARALLELS BETWEEN ALL FOUR TEXTS</h2>
        
        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>1. Memory/The Past as Inescapable Force</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Antoinette haunted by childhood trauma, mother's madness. Rochester haunted by rumors about Antoinette's past.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Okonkwo haunted by father's weakness, tries to escape it through excessive masculinity. Igbo people's past destroyed by colonialism.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther carries memory of 18 years of work in her quilt. Play = act of recovering erased historical memory ("Unidentified Negro Seamstress").</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> <span class="critical">Entire play = memory.</span> Tom the character trapped by past, Tom the narrator STILL trapped (creating play proves it). Amanda lives in memories of Southern belle youth. Laura's memories (Blue Roses) = safe haven.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> All four texts show <span class="critical">characters haunted by the past</span>, unable to move forward. Memory = both refuge AND prison.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>2. Illusions/Dreams vs. Harsh Reality</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Rochester's illusion that he can control Antoinette shatters. Antoinette's final "freedom" = madness (illusion).</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Igbo people's illusion that they can coexist with Christianity shatters. Okonkwo's dream of warrior glory = obsolete.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther's American Dream (beauty salon) = shattered by George. Mrs. Van Buren's "perfect" life = hollow illusion.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> Tom: "I give you truth in the pleasant disguise of illusion." <span class="critical">Entire play = dreamlike, non-realistic.</span> Amanda lives in illusions (gentleman caller fantasy). Laura's glass menagerie = escape into perfect, immortal objects. Jim's kiss = momentary illusion that shatters.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> All four texts explore <span class="critical">tension between comforting illusions and painful reality</span>. Characters create fantasy worlds to escape harsh truths.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>3. Oppressive Systems Destroy Individual Lives</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> British colonialism + patriarchal marriage → Antoinette loses identity, freedom, sanity.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> British colonialism → Igbo culture destroyed, Okonkwo commits suicide.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> American racism + capitalism + sexism → Esther's dream stolen, trapped in poverty.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> Great Depression + gender expectations + family obligations → Tom feels trapped, Laura can't survive in world, Amanda desperate. <span class="critical">Economic hardship compounds family dysfunction.</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> <span class="critical">Large oppressive systems crush individuals</span>, no matter how hard they try to resist. Personal agency = limited by structural forces.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>4. Marriage/Family as Trap (Especially for Women)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Antoinette's marriage → loses money, name, freedom, sanity. Christophine refuses marriage to keep independence.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Igbo women have limited power. Okonkwo beats wives, sees them as property.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther forced to marry for respectability → George steals everything. Mrs. Van Buren trapped in loveless marriage despite wealth.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> Amanda's marriage ended in abandonment → she's desperate, controlling. She projects marriage fantasy onto Laura (who's terrified of it). Family obligations trap Tom (can't leave without guilt). <span class="critical">Father's abandonment haunts entire family.</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> Marriage/family = <span class="critical">economic/social transaction that exploits women</span> and traps men in breadwinner roles. Love ≠ freedom.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>5. Escape - Desire vs. Impossibility</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Antoinette's only "escape" = madness/death (fire). No real escape possible.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Okonkwo's suicide = futile attempt to escape colonialism. No escape from cultural destruction.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther tries to escape through work/dreams → blocked at every turn. Small escape = female community (Mrs. Dickson).</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> <span class="critical">Tom constantly seeks escape</span> (fire escape, movies, alcohol, smoking, poetry, merchant marines) but all mechanisms are CYCLICAL → trap him. He physically leaves at end BUT creating play = still trapped in memories. <strong>Laura escapes into glass menagerie, Amanda into memories.</strong></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> All four texts explore <span class="critical">desire for escape vs. impossibility of true freedom</span>. Physical escape ≠ psychological escape. Characters trapped by external circumstances AND internal psychologies.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>6. Identity Crisis Under Oppression</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> "What am I doing in this place and who am I?" - Antoinette loses name, self.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Okonkwo's identity = warrior/achiever → colonialism makes this obsolete.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther's identity = seamstress/dreamer → history only sees "Unidentified Negro Seamstress."</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> Tom's identity crisis = torn between duty to family and desire for independence. "I'm leading a double life" (Scene 3). Laura's identity = fragile, otherworldly (Blue Roses, glass menagerie). Amanda clings to past identity (Southern belle) because present self is unbearable.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> When your <span class="critical">culture/value system/family is attacked or limiting, you lose sense of self.</span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>7. Language & Communication Failures</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Rochester and Antoinette = cultural/emotional gulf, can't understand each other.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> British missionaries and Igbo people = linguistic/cultural barriers, talk past each other.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther and George's romance = built on lies (he can't write, hires prostitute). Esther "reads" people through fabric instead of words.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> Tom and Amanda constantly miscommunicate (she doesn't trust him, he feels suffocated). Jim and Laura = finally communicate (Blue Roses memory) but Jim's engagement revelation = communication shatters. <span class="critical">Characters speak but don't connect.</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> <span class="critical">Miscommunication = tragedy.</span> People speak different languages (literal + metaphorical), can't bridge gaps.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="parallel-box">
            <h3>8. Fragility & Breaking</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Antoinette = fragile, breaks under pressure (goes mad). Marriage = breaking point.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Igbo society = complex but fragile, shatters under colonial pressure. Okonkwo = rigid, breaks rather than bends.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Esther's quilt = torn open, dream shattered. Fabric = delicate, can be ripped.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> <span class="critical">Glass menagerie = central symbol of fragility.</span> When Tom fights with Amanda, he shatters glass animals (Scene 3). When Jim dances with Laura, unicorn's horn breaks off. Laura herself = "like a piece of translucent glass touched by light" (stage direction). One false move = entire shimmering universe shatters.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> All four texts use <span class="critical">imagery of fragility and breaking</span> to show how vulnerable dreams/identities/relationships are. Beautiful things = too delicate to survive harsh reality.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- DIFFERENCES SECTION -->
        <h2>⚡ KEY DIFFERENCES BETWEEN THE FOUR TEXTS</h2>
        
        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>1. NARRATIVE STRUCTURE & PERSPECTIVE</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Text</th>
                    <th>Structure</th>
                    <th>Effect</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>WSS</strong></td>
                    <td>Multiple 1st person narrators (Antoinette, Rochester, Grace)</td>
                    <td>Fragmented, unreliable → mimics madness, no stable truth</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TFA</strong></td>
                    <td>3rd person omniscient, linear chronology</td>
                    <td>Authoritative, documentary → preserves Igbo culture objectively</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>IA</strong></td>
                    <td>Stage play, episodic scenes, realistic dialogue</td>
                    <td>Intimate, performance-based → emphasizes human connection</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TGM</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Tom = narrator AND character</span> (dual role)</td>
                    <td>Self-reflexive, dreamlike → memory filters everything, multiple layers of illusion</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Key difference:</strong> TGM is MOST explicitly about the act of storytelling itself. Tom addresses audience directly, manipulates stage effects. WSS has unreliable narrators but doesn't break 4th wall. TFA pretends to be objective. IA is realistic stage play.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>2. TONE & ENDING</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Text</th>
                    <th>Tone</th>
                    <th>Ending</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>WSS</strong></td>
                    <td>Gothic, dreamlike, psychological horror</td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Fully tragic:</span> Antoinette goes mad, sets fire (implied death)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TFA</strong></td>
                    <td>Epic, tragic, elegiac (mourning)</td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Tragic but dignified:</span> Okonkwo's suicide = resistance</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>IA</strong></td>
                    <td>Realistic, warm, bittersweet</td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Semi-hopeful:</span> Esther survives, dignity in labor, female community</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TGM</strong></td>
                    <td>Nostalgic, sentimental, poignant, tragicomic</td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Ambiguously tragic:</span> Tom leaves physically BUT still trapped emotionally (creating play = proof). Laura blows out candles. Paradoxical ending.</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Why differences?</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>WSS (1966) & TFA (1958): Written closer to colonial trauma → raw tragedy, no hope</li>
                <li>IA (2003): Retrospective historical view → finds resilience in survival</li>
                <li>TGM (1944): Written during WWII, about Great Depression → <strong>nostalgic yet painful.</strong> Emphasizes psychological rather than political oppression. Ending = neither fully hopeful nor fully tragic (Tom escapes physically but not mentally).</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>3. TYPE OF OPPRESSION (Primary Focus)</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Text</th>
                    <th>Primary Oppression</th>
                    <th>Secondary Oppressions</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>WSS</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="colonialism">Colonial + Patriarchal</span></td>
                    <td>Racial (Creole liminality), Class</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TFA</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="colonialism">Colonial (cultural imperialism)</span></td>
                    <td>Gender (toxic masculinity), Generational</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>IA</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="gender">Intersectional (Race + Gender + Class)</span></td>
                    <td>Economic exploitation, Segregation</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TGM</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="power">Family/Psychological + Economic</span></td>
                    <td>Gender expectations, Great Depression poverty</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Key difference:</strong> TGM is <span class="critical">ONLY text where oppression = primarily familial/psychological</span> rather than explicitly colonial/racial/political. Tom's "trap" = family obligations + economic hardship, NOT colonial violence. This makes TGM feel more intimate, more about internal family dynamics. However, Great Depression context = structural economic oppression (similar to other texts' political oppressions).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>4. GENDER PERSPECTIVE</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Text</th>
                    <th>Gender Focus</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>WSS</strong></td>
                    <td>Explicitly feminist - centers female oppression in marriage + colonialism. Christophine = model of female independence.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TFA</strong></td>
                    <td>Male-centered (Okonkwo's story), BUT critiques toxic masculinity. Women have limited roles but some power (Chielo).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>IA</strong></td>
                    <td>Intersectional feminist - race + gender + class compound. Shows ALL women trapped differently (Esther, Mrs. Van Buren, Mayme).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TGM</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Explores BOTH male and female traps.</span> Tom = trapped by breadwinner role, family duty, masculine expectations. Laura & Amanda = trapped by feminine expectations (marriage, domesticity). <strong>Sympathetic to all characters' struggles.</strong></td>
                </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin-top: 15px;"><strong>Key difference:</strong> TGM shows how <span class="critical">gender expectations trap BOTH men and women</span> (though differently). Tom must be breadwinner, can't pursue poetry/dreams. Laura must be marriageable, can't just be herself. Amanda lost everything when husband left. Father felt need to escape. All trapped by gender roles.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>5. RELATIONSHIP TO MEMORY/PAST</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> Past = source of trauma that characters can't escape. Memory = weapon (Rochester uses rumors about Antoinette's past to control her).</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> Past = lost golden age that can never be recovered. Memory = elegy for destroyed culture. Achebe DOCUMENTS past to preserve it.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Past = erased (photo caption: "Unidentified"). Memory = act of historical recovery. Play RESTORES past.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> <span class="critical">Past = THE ENTIRE PLAY.</span> Memory = both Tom's trap AND his attempted liberation. Play = cathartic act to purge memories (but paradoxically proves he can't escape them). <strong>Most self-reflexive about memory as narrative construct.</strong></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> TGM is <span class="critical">ONLY text where memory itself = the PRIMARY subject and structure</span>, not just a theme. Tom announces "The play is memory" in opening line. Everything = filtered through his subjective recall.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>6. POSSIBILITY OF HOPE/RESISTANCE</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>WSS:</strong> <span class="critical">NO HOPE</span> - Antoinette's only "freedom" = madness/death. Fire = destruction, not liberation.</li>
                <li><strong>TFA:</strong> <span class="critical">NO HOPE</span> - Tradition destroyed, Okonkwo's suicide = futile. District Commissioner reduces his life to one paragraph.</li>
                <li><strong>IA:</strong> <span class="critical">SMALL HOPE</span> - Esther survives with skill, community. Dignity in labor. Female solidarity = survival strategy.</li>
                <li><strong>TGM:</strong> <span class="critical">AMBIGUOUS HOPE</span> - Tom achieves physical escape (leaves family, joins merchant marines). BUT emotionally still trapped (creating play = he hasn't moved on). Is the play itself = successful catharsis (hope) or proof of eternal entrapment (no hope)? <strong>Williams leaves this open.</strong></li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Key difference:</strong> TGM's ending = <span class="critical">most ambiguous of the four</span>. Not clearly tragic (Tom does escape physically) but not clearly hopeful (he's still haunted). Asks: Can we ever truly escape our memories? The play suggests both yes (Tom got away) and no (he's still telling the story).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="difference-box">
            <h3>7. SYMBOLISM & IMAGERY</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Text</th>
                    <th>Central Symbols</th>
                    <th>Effect</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>WSS</strong></td>
                    <td>Fire, mirrors, red dress, Coulibri estate</td>
                    <td>Gothic, psychological - emphasize madness, fragmentation</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TFA</strong></td>
                    <td>Yam, locusts, wrestling, kola nut, drums</td>
                    <td>Cultural symbols from Igbo tradition - document authentic culture</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>IA</strong></td>
                    <td>Crazy quilt, fabric, intimate apparel, Panama Canal letters</td>
                    <td>Material objects - emphasize labor, craftsmanship, hidden work</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>TGM</strong></td>
                    <td><span class="critical">Glass menagerie, unicorn, Blue Roses, fire escape, candles, moon, movies, typewriter</span></td>
