                <li><strong>IA:</strong> Play = act of recovering erased memory ("Unidentified Negro Seamstress"). Esther carries 18 years in her quilt until it's torn open.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Synthesis:</strong> Unlike TGM, where memory = explicit subject, WSS/TFA/IA embed memory thematically. But all four texts show: [critical insight about memory as trap]. [Connect back to thesis].</p>
        </div>

        <h3>CONCLUSION (1 paragraph, ~200 words)</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Restate thesis</strong> in fresh language</li>
            <li><strong>Broader significance:</strong> What do these 4 texts teach us?
                <ul>
                    <li>"Together, these four texts—spanning different cultures, time periods, and forms of oppression—reveal a universal human struggle: the tension between the comforting illusions we create to survive and the harsh realities we must ultimately face. Whether trapped by colonial violence (WSS, TFA), racist capitalism (IA), or family obligations and memory itself (TGM), individuals seek refuge in dreams, memories, and fantasy worlds. Yet these illusions = both salvation and damnation."</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Final powerful thought:</strong>
                <ul>
                    <li>"Perhaps the act of storytelling itself—Rhys giving voice to the 'madwoman,' Achebe preserving Igbo culture, Nottage recovering the 'Unidentified' seamstress, Williams transforming memory into theater—represents the only escape that matters: not physical flight, but the refusal to let oppressive systems determine the narrative. Even in destruction, these characters refuse to disappear."</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <!-- TIPS SECTION -->
        <h2>✅ CRITICAL TIPS FOR 4-TEXT ESSAY</h2>
        
        <div style="background: #c8e6c9; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border: 2px solid #4caf50; margin: 20px 0;">
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">DO:</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ Choose 2-3 MAJOR themes to focus on (can't cover everything with 4 texts!)</li>
                <li>✅ Use BEST quotes only (1-2 per text maximum per paragraph)</li>
                <li>✅ SYNTHESIZE across texts rather than discussing each separately</li>
                <li>✅ Make connections explicit ("Like WSS, TGM also..." or "Unlike TFA, IA instead...")</li>
                <li>✅ Use transition phrases constantly: "Similarly," "In contrast," "While all four texts..."</li>
                <li>✅ Stay focused on your thesis - every paragraph connects back</li>
                <li>✅ Analyze quotes (explain HOW they support your point)</li>
                <li>✅ Be precise with literary terminology</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #ffcdd2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border: 2px solid #e53935; margin: 20px 0;">
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">DON'T:</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>❌ Try to discuss EVERY theme in EVERY text (you'll run out of time!)</li>
                <li>❌ Write separate sections for each text (must COMPARE throughout)</li>
                <li>❌ Plot summary instead of analysis</li>
                <li>❌ Quotes without explanation</li>
                <li>❌ Weak thesis - be SPECIFIC and ARGUABLE</li>
                <li>❌ Forget to mention one of the 4 texts (must use all of them!)</li>
                <li>❌ Panic if you realize you can't cover everything - that's normal with 4 texts!</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>STRONG THESIS EXAMPLES FOR 4 TEXTS:</h3>
        
        <div style="background: #fff9c4; padding: 15px; border-left: 5px solid #fbc02d; margin: 15px 0;">
            <p><strong>Example 1 (Memory focus):</strong> "While Rhys, Achebe, Nottage, and Williams all depict characters haunted by memory and seeking escape, they employ radically different narrative strategies to explore this theme: Rhys's fragmented narration mimics psychological breakdown; Achebe's objective third-person mourns cultural destruction; Nottage's realistic theater recovers erased histories; and Williams's self-reflexive memory play questions whether escape from the past is ever truly possible, ultimately suggesting that memory = both prison and the only freedom available."</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #fff9c4; padding: 15px; border-left: 5px solid #fbc02d; margin: 15px 0;">
            <p><strong>Example 2 (Illusion focus):</strong> "All four texts reveal how marginalized individuals create fragile illusions to survive oppressive circumstances—Antoinette's madness, Okonkwo's warrior identity, Esther's American Dream, Laura's glass menagerie—but differ fundamentally in their visions of what happens when these illusions shatter: WSS and TFA offer no hope beyond destruction; IA finds resilience in survival and community; and TGM presents the paradox that even physical escape cannot free us from our memories."</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #fff9c4; padding: 15px; border-left: 5px solid #fbc02d; margin: 15px 0;">
            <p><strong>Example 3 (Systems focus):</strong> "Though Rhys and Achebe depict explicit colonial violence, Nottage explores racist capitalism, and Williams examines family/economic pressures, all four texts share a central insight: oppressive systems—whether political or personal—destroy individuals by erasing their identities, yet the act of storytelling itself becomes a form of resistance, preserving voices that history attempted to silence."</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #e1f5fe; padding: 25px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 30px 0; text-align: center; border: 3px solid #0288d1;">
            <h2 style="color: #01579b; margin-bottom: 15px;">YOU CAN DO THIS!</h2>
